data: {"type":"handoff","handoff":{"text":"{\"v\":1,\"hash\":\"b9c4a57381ea9ddb699ee666755c58f30b5b91b61b304330fddf621f633a2b36\",\"at\":1793710612847,\"retried\":[],\"merged\":{\"byChunk\":[{\"chunkId\":1,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"1.1\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 (July 16–24, 1969) was the American spaceflight that first landed humans on the Moon and the fifth crewed Apollo mission.\"},{\"id\":\"1.2\",\"text\":\"The crew was Commander Neil Armstrong, Command Module Pilot Michael Collins, and Lunar Module Pilot Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin, all on their second and final spaceflight.\"},{\"id\":\"1.3\",\"text\":\"The Saturn V launched from Kennedy Space Center on July 16, 1969 at 13:32 UTC.\"},{\"id\":\"1.4\",\"text\":\"The command module housed the three astronauts and was the only part to return to Earth; the service module provided propulsion, power, oxygen, and water.\"},{\"id\":\"1.5\",\"text\":\"Armstrong and Aldrin landed the LM Eagle in the Sea of Tranquility on July 20 while Collins remained in lunar orbit aboard Columbia.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Lunar Module Eagle\",\"Sea of Tranquility\",\"Columbia\",\"Apollo program\"]},{\"chunkId\":2,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"2.1\",\"text\":\"Armstrong walked on the Moon about six hours after landing, with Aldrin following nineteen minutes later.\"},{\"id\":\"2.2\",\"text\":\"The two spent about two and a half hours on the surface, planting a flag, speaking with President Nixon, and collecting 21.5 kg of lunar material.\"},{\"id\":\"2.3\",\"text\":\"The crew rejoined Collins in lunar orbit and returned to Earth, splashing down in the Pacific Ocean on July 24.\"},{\"id\":\"2.4\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 was the culmination of the Space Race, a Cold War competition between the United States and the Soviet Union.\"},{\"id\":\"2.5\",\"text\":\"President John F. Kennedy set the goal in May 1961 of landing a man on the Moon before the decade ended; the program overcame the fatal Apollo 1 fire in January 1967.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 11\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"Mercury program\",\"Gemini program\"]},{\"chunkId\":3,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"3.1\",\"text\":\"Despite early spaceflight leads, the Soviet Union could not match the Saturn V; its uncrewed Luna 15 crashed on the Moon on July 21 while Armstrong and Aldrin were on the surface.\"},{\"id\":\"3.2\",\"text\":\"Armstrong's moonwalk was broadcast live to an estimated 600 million viewers, roughly one-fifth of the world's population.\"},{\"id\":\"3.3\",\"text\":\"Stepping onto the lunar surface, Armstrong declared, \\\"That's one small step for [a] man, one giant leap for mankind.\\\"\"},{\"id\":\"3.4\",\"text\":\"Lunar samples led to the identification of three previously unrecognized minerals, and surface instruments kept returning data for years.\"},{\"id\":\"3.5\",\"text\":\"The crew received ticker-tape parades, a world goodwill tour, and the Presidential Medal of Freedom; Columbia is preserved at the National Air and Space Museum.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Luna 15\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Columbia\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Eagle\",\"United States\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\"]},{\"chunkId\":4,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"4.1\",\"text\":\"On October 4, 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, the first artificial satellite, which surprised the world and fueled fears about Soviet capabilities.\"},{\"id\":\"4.2\",\"text\":\"Sputnik's success suggested the USSR could potentially deliver nuclear weapons over intercontinental distances, challenging American claims of superiority.\"},{\"id\":\"4.3\",\"text\":\"Sputnik sparked the Sputnik crisis and ignited the Space Race between the two superpowers.\"},{\"id\":\"4.4\",\"text\":\"President Dwight D. Eisenhower responded by creating NASA and initiating Project Mercury, which aimed to place a man into Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"4.5\",\"text\":\"Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space and first to orbit Earth on April 12, 1961; Alan Shepard became the first American in space on May 5, 1961 on a suborbital flight.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"Sputnik crisis\",\"Space Race\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"John F. Kennedy\"]},{\"chunkId\":5,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"5.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy, Eisenhower's successor, chose a challenge beyond existing rocket capabilities so both countries would start from an equal position.\"},{\"id\":\"5.2\",\"text\":\"A crewed Moon mission was presented as the challenge that would serve this purpose.\"},{\"id\":\"5.3\",\"text\":\"On May 25, 1961, Kennedy told Congress the nation should commit to landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the decade ends.\"},{\"id\":\"5.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said no single space project would be more impressive to mankind or more important for long-range space exploration, and none more difficult or expensive.\"},{\"id\":\"5.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed accelerating lunar spacecraft development, developing liquid and solid fuel boosters, and funding engine development and unmanned exploration.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\"]},{\"chunkId\":6,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"6.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy's speech implies that reaching the Moon would be a national effort, since all Americans must work to put one man there.\"},{\"id\":\"6.2\",\"text\":\"On September 12, 1962, Kennedy gave a speech to about 40,000 people at Rice University football stadium in Houston, Texas.\"},{\"id\":\"6.3\",\"text\":\"The speech argues outer space currently has no strife, prejudice, or national conflict, and its hazards threaten everyone alike.\"},{\"id\":\"6.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy asks why the Moon should be the goal, comparing it to climbing mountains, flying the Atlantic, and Rice playing Texas.\"},{\"id\":\"6.5\",\"text\":\"The refrain 'We choose to go to the Moon' is a widely quoted line from the speech's middle portion.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Rice University football stadium\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Moon\",\"outer space\",\"Atlantic\",\"Rice\",\"Texas\"]},{\"chunkId\":7,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"7.1\",\"text\":\"The Moon goal was chosen because it is hard, to organize and measure the best energies and skills, and the nation intends to win.\"},{\"id\":\"7.2\",\"text\":\"The proposed program faced widespread opposition; Norbert Wiener, an MIT mathematician, dubbed it a \\\"moondoggle.\\\"\"},{\"id\":\"7.3\",\"text\":\"The effort to land a man on the Moon was named Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"7.4\",\"text\":\"In June 1961 Kennedy proposed a joint Moon landing to Premier Nikita Khrushchev, who declined; Kennedy repeated the offer to the UN General Assembly on September 20, 1963.\"},{\"id\":\"7.5\",\"text\":\"The joint Moon mission idea was ultimately abandoned after Kennedy's death, and an early key decision was lunar orbit rendezvous over direct ascent and Earth orbit rendezvous.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"Soviet Union\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\",\"Moon\"]},{\"chunkId\":8,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"8.1\",\"text\":\"In July 1962, NASA head James Webb announced that lunar orbit rendezvous would be used for Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"8.2\",\"text\":\"The Apollo spacecraft had three major parts: the command module, service module, and lunar module.\"},{\"id\":\"8.3\",\"text\":\"Only the command module returned to Earth; the service module supplied propulsion, power, oxygen, and water.\"},{\"id\":\"8.4\",\"text\":\"The lunar module had a descent stage for landing on the Moon and an ascent stage to return to lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"8.5\",\"text\":\"Apollo technologies and techniques were developed by Project Gemini, and NASA adopted new semiconductor advances for Apollo.\"}],\"entities\":[\"James Webb\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Saturn V\",\"command module\",\"service module\",\"lunar module\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer\"]},{\"chunkId\":9,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"9.1\",\"text\":\"Apollo 1 fire on January 27, 1967 killed astronauts Gus Grissom, Ed White, and Roger B. Chaffee, abruptly halting Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"9.2\",\"text\":\"In October 1968, Apollo 7 evaluated the command module in Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.3\",\"text\":\"In December 1968, Apollo 8 tested the command module in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.4\",\"text\":\"In March 1969, Apollo 9 tested the lunar module in Earth orbit; in May, Apollo 10 conducted a 'dress rehearsal' in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.5\",\"text\":\"By July 1969, everything was ready for Apollo 11 to make the final step onto the Moon.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Project Apollo\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\",\"Apollo 11\"]}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Lunar Module Eagle\",\"Sea of Tranquility\",\"Columbia\",\"Apollo program\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"Mercury program\",\"Gemini program\",\"Luna 15\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Eagle\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"Sputnik crisis\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\",\"Rice University football stadium\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"outer space\",\"Atlantic\",\"Rice\",\"Texas\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\",\"James Webb\",\"Apollo\",\"Project Gemini\",\"command module\",\"service module\",\"lunar module\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\"],\"findingCount\":9}}","seal":"0a880428bc2085109bcffd4afa33cf46f9bef174219d8eec6a4de4c29f4dce1c"}}

